  "source_findings": {
    "lifetime_enforcement": "The managed capacity provider validates the requested lifetime against the profile. Launcher enrollment carries no lifetime or deadline. ApiExecutor inherits the process-wide PoolWorker lifetime clock, which defaults to 21600 seconds. The loop's clock starts with the executor rather than being persisted with the incarnation.",
    "credentials": "Run credentials are per attempt. Managed control keys are derived by installation, root version, scope, incarnation and resource UID, without a timestamp expiry. The API validates hello freshness and increasing sequence and renews the 20-second owner lease. The local SDK and hello fixture uses synthetic credentials and does not run the API lease.",
    "idle": "Ordinary terminal ACK releases occupancy. The 90-second linger gates readiness reuse without a scheduled idle ping. Physical idle drain is a separate PoolWorker policy. Local Miniflare does not run that API-side idle loop.",
    "upgrade": "Closing revision admission initiates drain independently of nominal lifetime. Active attempts use a 300-second drain grace and the upgrade job retains its 600-second deadline.",
    "memory": "RSS is aggregate workerd process memory. sys.modules counts cover the fixture child. Per-isolate JS and Wasm heap and hosted limit behavior require separate qualification."
  },
  "verdict": "Hold preset B. The 900-second value reached the provider reservation but the release's fixed four-slot journal budget stopped the workload at approximately 180 seconds.",
